---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";

// Issue #1105: "Life-stage modes." Facts come from the shipped mode system
// (issue #188): lib/domain/models/lifecycle_mode.dart,
// supabase/migrations/20260909000000_profile_modes_and_cycle_overrides.sql,
// and the app copy in lib/l10n/app_en.arb. No medical claims — modes change
// what the app computes and shows, nothing else.

const description =
  "Five life-stage modes — Period Tracking, Conceive, Pregnancy, " +
  "Perimenopause, and Postpartum. Switch any time; nothing you logged is " +
  "ever rewritten.";
---

<BaseLayout title="Life-stage modes" description={description}>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Life-stage modes</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      A cycle tracker that assumes every month is the same kind of month is
      wrong three or four times a decade. A profile's life-stage mode tells
      lunarlog what to compute and show — and switching is free, reversible,
      and never touches what you logged.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>The five modes</h2>
      <!-- cite: the closed mode set and its labels, lib/domain/models/lifecycle_mode.dart (LifecycleMode.label; the server CHECK constraint comment); supabase/migrations/20260909000000_profile_modes_and_cycle_overrides.sql. -->
      <p>
        Every profile runs in exactly one of five modes, set from the
        profile editor:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Period Tracking</strong> — the default: cycles, flow,
          symptoms, and the estimates built from them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Conceive</strong> — adds an estimated fertile window and a
          daily-chance reading, each framed with the research behind it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pregnancy</strong> — pauses the period estimate and counts
          by week from an estimated due date.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Perimenopause</strong> — pauses the period estimate while
          cycles are still logged and kept.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Postpartum</strong> — pauses the period estimate and
          counts the days of postpartum, with a gentle offer to log when a
          first bleed arrives.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <!-- cite: pregnancy week counter (lib/l10n/app_en.arb pregnancyWeekTitle, issue #192); postpartum day counter (postpartumDayTitle, issue #455); the paused-estimate state naming the mode (predictionsSuppressedTitle / predictionsSuppressedByModeBody, issue #528); conceive window copy (conceiveWindowLabel, conceivePeakDay). -->
      <figure class="screenshot-figure">
        <Screenshot
          screen="life-stage"
          device="iphone-67"
          theme="light"
          alt="The profile editor's life-stage mode chooser, open, with the five modes listed."
        />
        <figcaption>Choosing a life-stage mode in the profile editor.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Switching never rewrites your history</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/domain/models/lifecycle_mode.dart ("Switching is free and reversible: tracked data is kept... a switch never deletes or rewrites day_entries/observations"); issue #188 A2-32. -->
      <p>
        Changing modes changes what the app computes and displays — nothing
        else. Entries, symptoms, tags, and notes are all kept exactly as
        they were, every switch recomputes estimates from the same history,
        and you can switch back whenever life changes again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How the modes read</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 (fertile-window estimate is a calendar-method back-calculation and "must not be used to prevent pregnancy"); lib/l10n/app_en.arb conceivePeakDay (the study framing and percentage). -->
      <p>
        In Conceive mode, the fertile window is an estimate derived from the
        profile's own logged history, and the daily-chance figure arrives
        with its source study named in the app. It is information, not
        medical advice — and it must not be used to prevent pregnancy.
        Pregnancy, Perimenopause, and Postpartum modes say openly that the
        period estimate is paused, instead of silently showing a stale
        prediction.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Who is reading also matters</h2>
      <!-- cite: the orthogonal care-mode axis, lib/domain/models/profile_mode.dart (standard/teen/caregiver/irregular; issue #131) and lifecycle_mode.dart's orthogonality note; teen-mode framing copy in lib/l10n/app_en.arb subjectTeenModeDialogBody. -->
      <p>
        Modes change what is computed; separately, a profile's reading
        level — standard, teen, or supported care framing — changes the
        vocabulary the app uses with whoever is reading. The two axes
        compose: a teen profile in Period Tracking mode, or an adult
        profile in Perimenopause mode with a caregiver alongside. A teen
        profile's reader level is suggested once, never forced.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      Modes sync with everything else a profile shares — see
      <a href="/family-sharing">family sharing and roles</a>, or how
      <a href="/tracking">estimates show their confidence</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
